@node Obtaining Emacsvox
@section Before You Begin

Install @code{git}@comma{} @code{make}@comma{} GNU Texinfo (which provides @code{makeinfo})@comma{} and
@uref{https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/download.html, Emacs 31 or newer}.  The recommended Omnivox speech server also needs the
Rust toolchain@comma{} Python 3@comma{} a C and C++ toolchain@comma{} CMake@comma{} and Clang with libclang.

To check the Emacs found by your shell@comma{} run:

@example
emacs --version
@end example

The first line should begin with @code{GNU Emacs 31} or a higher version number.  If
the command is not found or reports an older version@comma{} install a supported Emacs
or locate its executable.  You can check a specific executable in the same way:

@example
/path/to/emacs-31/bin/emacs --version
@end example

On GNU/Linux@comma{} install @code{pkg-config} and the ALSA development headers.  On
macOS@comma{} install the Xcode command-line tools.  WSL2 has additional Docker and
Windows requirements described in @ref{Building Omnivox For WSL2}.

Choose a permanent parent directory and clone Emacsvox and Omnivox beside one
another:

@example
cd /path/where/you/keep/source
git clone https://github.com/bartbunting/emacsvox
git clone https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox
@end example

The Emacsvox checkout is the installation directory.  It builds in place and
does not create a second tree to copy elsewhere.

@node Building And Testing The Speech Server
@section Install And Check Omnivox

On GNU/Linux or macOS@comma{} build and install Omnivox from its checkout:

@example
cd /path/where/you/keep/source/omnivox
make build
make install
omnivox --check
@end example

@code{make install} normally places the executable below @code{~/.cargo/bin}.  Make sure
that directory is on @code{PATH} before running the check.  No proprietary speech
runtime is needed for the recommended GNU/Linux or macOS route.

Under WSL2@comma{} build the staged Windows runtime from a clean Emacsvox checkout:

@example
cd /path/where/you/keep/source/emacsvox
make windows-omnivox
./servers/omnivox --check
@end example

The check plays a tone followed by speech.  When you hear both@comma{} the speech
server is ready.  If either is missing@comma{} use
@ref{Backend Diagnostics And Ownership,Backend Diagnostics And Responsibilities}
before continuing.  @xref{Speech Backends} contains the complete platform
matrix@comma{} build details@comma{} and compatibility alternatives.  Native Windows Emacs
support remains a preview; consult that matrix before choosing it for a first
installation.

@node Quick Installation
@section Build And Start Emacsvox

@enumerate
@item
Enter the Emacsvox checkout:

@example
cd /path/where/you/keep/source/emacsvox
@end example

@item
Select Emacs 31 or newer.  If it is not the system @code{emacs}@comma{} create the
ignored local settings file:

@example
cp local.mk.example local.mk
@end example

Then set @code{EMACS} in @code{local.mk}:

@example
EMACS=/path/to/emacs-31/bin/emacs
@end example

The earlier @code{emacs --version} command checks your shell's default.  Verify
the executable selected by the Emacsvox build separately:

@example
make check-emacs
@end example

@code{make check-emacs} uses @code{local.mk} when present@comma{} prints the selected
executable and version@comma{} and confirms whether Emacsvox supports it.

@item
Configure and compile Emacsvox:

@example
make all
@end example

A successful build returns to the shell without an error.  Emacsvox must be
compiled with the same supported Emacs selected in the previous step.

@item
Run the non-audible launcher preflight from the Emacsvox checkout:

@example
./bin/emacsvox --diagnose
@end example

This checks the checkout@comma{} Emacs@comma{} and speech server without playing audio or
opening interactive Emacs.  A successful report names Emacs 31 or newer@comma{}
the @code{omnivox} speech server@comma{} and an isolated @code{-Q} startup.  If it reports a
path or version problem@comma{} correct that item and run the command again.

@item
Check the complete launch path with sound:

@example
./bin/emacsvox --check
@end example

The launcher prints what it selected@comma{} then plays a tone and speech.  Hearing
both means the complete speech-server launch path is ready.

@item
Start a clean Emacsvox session:

@example
./bin/emacsvox
@end example

The launcher uses Omnivox and the selected Emacs@comma{} but does not load site
files or your personal Emacs init file.  This gives you a known starting
point without changing an existing Emacs setup.
@end enumerate

If a command needs an explicit path@comma{} @code{./bin/emacsvox --help} describes the
@code{EMACS}@comma{} @code{TTS_PROGRAM}@comma{} and @code{EMACSVOX_DIR} overrides.  Arguments after @code{--}
are passed to Emacs.

@node Confirming First Speech
@section Confirming First Speech

You will know the isolated start succeeded when it speaks an introduction
ending with:

@quotation
I am completely operational@comma{} and all my circuits are functioning perfectly!

@end quotation

The preceding overview key and version number can change.  The sentence above
is the stable first-speech checkpoint.  A startup auditory icon may also play@comma{}
depending on the available audio support.

You now have a working Emacsvox session.  Try these checks before connecting
it to your personal configuration:

@enumerate
@item
Type @kbd{M-x tts-speak-version RET}.  The live speech adapter should speak its
version response.

@item
Type @kbd{C-h v tts-program RET}.  The displayed current value should name
@code{omnivox}.

@item
Use @kbd{C-n} and @kbd{C-p} to move over lines and confirm ordinary spoken
navigation.  Type @kbd{M-x emacsvox-describe-emacsvox RET} for the Emacsvox
overview.  @xref{Basic Usage}@comma{} for the tested starter commands.
@end enumerate

When these checks pass@comma{} continue to @ref{Basic Usage} for a short guided
first session@comma{} or use @ref{Persistent Startup} to connect Emacsvox to your
normal Emacs configuration.

@node Recovering From No Speech
@section Recovering From No Speech

If speech did not start@comma{} keep the isolated launcher as your known starting
point and find the first command below that did not work:

@table @asis
@item @code{./bin/emacsvox --diagnose} fails
No audio or interactive Emacs has started.  Follow the reported selection:
set @code{EMACS}@comma{} correct @code{EMACS=} in @code{local.mk}@comma{} or install Emacs 31
or newer when the Emacs check fails.  When the speech backend is not
executable@comma{} finish installing Omnivox@comma{} put it on @code{PATH}@comma{} or set
@code{OMNIVOX_PROGRAM} to its absolute executable.  Under WSL2@comma{} rebuild or
verify the staged runtime described in @ref{Building Omnivox For WSL2}.
@item The diagnostic passes@comma{} but @code{--check} errors or is inaudible
The speech server needs attention before Emacs configuration is relevant.
Follow
@ref{Backend Diagnostics And Ownership,Backend Diagnostics And Responsibilities} until the check produces both its tone
and speech.
@item The audible check passes@comma{} but the isolated startup sentence is absent
Run @code{./bin/emacsvox --diagnose} again from the same checkout and shell.
Remove an unintended @code{TTS_PROGRAM} override if the report does not select
@code{omnivox}.  Then request an Emacs startup backtrace with:

@example
./bin/emacsvox -- --debug-init
@end example

An error saying @code{Could not find speech server executable} means the
backend visible during that start cannot be executed.  Correct the reported
backend path rather than adding more init-file configuration.  For an Omnivox
process error@comma{} inspect the private log directory described below.
@item The startup sentence is heard@comma{} but speech later stops
If Emacs reports that an Emacsvox speech server exited@comma{} inspect the Omnivox
session log and type @kbd{M-x tts-restart RET} after correcting the backend
problem.  Logs are below @code{$XDG_STATE_HOME/emacsvox/omnivox}@comma{} defaulting to
@code{~/.local/state/emacsvox/omnivox}.  Spoken text is omitted unless explicit
text logging is enabled.
@item The isolated launcher works@comma{} but a customized Emacs start does not
Remove or comment out the Emacsvox environment and @code{load-file} forms from
the personal init file.  This is the rollback: it leaves the checkout and the
known-good @code{./bin/emacsvox} path intact.  Re-add the forms in the order
shown in @ref{Persistent Startup}; backend selection must happen before setup
is loaded.
@end table

@node Persistent Startup
@section Persistent Startup

After @ref{Confirming First Speech} succeeds@comma{} add these forms to your Emacs
init file@comma{} replacing the example path with the absolute path of the checkout:

@lisp
(setenv "TTS_PROGRAM" "omnivox")
(load-file "/path/to/emacsvox/lisp/emacsvox-setup.el")
@end lisp

Keep the forms in this order: Emacsvox selects and starts the speech server while
loading setup.  A shell alternative is to export the server name before
launching Emacs:

@example
export TTS_PROGRAM=omnivox
@end example

Keep the explicit @code{load-file} form for a changing source checkout.  Its
setup entry point can warn and prefer source when newer Lisp would otherwise
be hidden by stale local byte-code.  A customized startup should speak the
same acceptance sentence as the isolated launcher.  If it does not@comma{} remove or
comment out the two init forms and return to the isolated launcher before
trying another change.

@node Basic Usage
@chapter Basic Usage

Now that Emacsvox is speaking@comma{} this chapter helps you become comfortable with
the essential keys and feedback.  It begins with a short guided session@comma{} then
introduces the reading and speech controls you are most likely to need.

@menu
* Overview::                     Understand the main kinds of Emacsvox feedback.
* Working::                      Understand feedback while editing and completing text.
* Reading::                      Reading chunks of information.
* Speech System::                Adjust speech for the way you are working.
* Voice-lock::                   Hear document and program structure through voice changes.
* Status Information::           Ask about the current buffer, position, and Emacs state.
@end menu

@section Key Notation And Emacs Words

Emacs documentation writes @kbd{C-} for the Control key and @kbd{M-} for the
Meta key@comma{} which is usually Alt or Option.  For example@comma{} @kbd{C-n} means
hold Control and press @kbd{n}.  A sequence such as @kbd{C-e l} means press
@kbd{C-e}@comma{} release it@comma{} and then press @kbd{l}.

@kbd{M-x} prompts for an Emacs command by name@comma{} and @key{RET} means the
Return or Enter key.  A @emph{buffer} is the Emacs workspace containing text or an
application.  @emph{Point} is the current editing position—the place affected by
movement and editing commands.

If Emacs itself is new to you@comma{} @kbd{C-h t} opens its interactive tutorial.
You can return to this chapter whenever the tutorial refers to buffers@comma{} point@comma{}
windows@comma{} or key sequences.

@section Your First Few Minutes

Try these actions in the clean session started during installation:

@enumerate
@item
Press @kbd{C-n} and @kbd{C-p} to move down and up by one line.  Emacsvox
speaks the line you reach.

@item
Press @kbd{C-e l} to hear the current line without moving.

@item
Press @kbd{C-e s} whenever you want to stop speech immediately.

@item
Press @kbd{C-h k} followed by any key to hear what that key does.

@item
Press @kbd{C-h m} to hear about the modes and commands available in the
current buffer.

@item
Press @kbd{C-h C-e} to open the Emacsvox overview.
@end enumerate

These commands are enough to begin exploring safely.  The rest of this chapter
adds reading controls and speech settings as you need them.  The generated
@ref{Emacsvox Keymaps,,,emacsvox-reference,Emacsvox Reference Manual} records the complete default keymaps@comma{} while live
help remains authoritative when a package or personal configuration changes a
binding.

@node Overview
@section Emacsvox Overview

Emacsvox adds spoken feedback to Emacs editing and to applications that run
inside Emacs.  Movement speaks the corresponding character@comma{} word@comma{} line@comma{} or
larger unit.  Commands announce useful results and state changes@comma{} while
application integrations present structures such as messages@comma{} search results@comma{}
tables@comma{} source code@comma{} and Web pages.

Voice Lock uses changes in speech characteristics to make text structure
audible.  For example@comma{} comments@comma{} quoted strings@comma{} reserved words@comma{} headings@comma{} and
links can sound different without an explanation being inserted into the text.
See @ref{Voice Lock} for configuration and @ref{Web Browsing} for a
task-oriented example in EWW@.

Pronunciation dictionaries let you adjust how a name@comma{} technical term@comma{} or
programming-language word is spoken.  A dictionary can apply everywhere or
only in a particular mode or file; see
@ref{emacsvox-pronounce,,,emacsvox-reference,Emacsvox Reference Manual}.

Short auditory icons and tones mark events such as opening or deleting a file@comma{}
finishing an action@comma{} receiving mail@comma{} or displaying completions.  They
supplement speech and become especially useful as familiar events no longer
need a full spoken explanation.  See
@ref{emacsvox-sounds,,,emacsvox-reference,Emacsvox Reference Manual} for the available sound support.

@node Working
@section Using Emacs Buffers

As you type@comma{} character echo can speak individual characters and word echo can
speak a word when you finish it.  Completion interfaces announce candidates@comma{}
the inserted completion@comma{} and the item currently selected.  Prompts and error
messages are spoken through the same Emacs feedback that appears on screen.

Most everyday feedback happens automatically.  Emacsvox also provides three
useful groups of explicit commands:

@itemize
@item
Reading commands speak a character@comma{} word@comma{} line@comma{} region@comma{} or larger portion of
a buffer without requiring you to move first.  Their names usually begin
with @code{emacsvox-speak-}@comma{} and their default keys use the main @kbd{C-e}
prefix.@footnote{The prefix is inherited from Emacspeak@comma{} where C-e was mnemonic
for the project name.}

@item
Speech-system commands change settings such as rate@comma{} punctuation@comma{} and echo.
Their names usually begin with @code{tts-}@comma{} and many are grouped under
@kbd{C-e d}.@footnote{Historically@comma{} d was mnemonic for DECtalk; the prefix now
contains engine-independent speech controls.}

@item
Help commands describe the active modes@comma{} available keys@comma{} and Emacsvox
integrations in the buffer where you are working.
@end itemize

The following sections introduce a practical starter set.  Use live Help or
the generated reference when you need the complete commands for a particular
application.

