@end table

Use @kbd{C-h m} or the generated keymap reference for server selection@comma{}
repeated-pattern cleanup@comma{} text splitting@comma{} language selection@comma{} and direct engine
controls.  Those specialist settings are intentionally outside this starter
guide.

@node Voice-lock
@section Voice Lock Mode

Voice Lock is enabled automatically in supported buffers.  It maps faces and
other structural text properties to speech personalities@comma{} allowing comments@comma{}
strings@comma{} headings@comma{} links@comma{} and other elements to sound distinct without adding
words to the document.

The exact personalities depend on the selected speech engine and your Aural
Presentation settings.  Try Voice Lock in source code@comma{} Org@comma{} Info@comma{} or EWW to
hear the active mode's structure.

To disable Voice Lock globally@comma{} run @kbd{M-x global-voice-lock-mode} or
add this form to your Emacs init file:

@lisp
(global-voice-lock-mode -1)
@end lisp

The current binding and command reference are in
@ref{Emacsvox Keymaps,,,emacsvox-reference,Emacsvox Reference Manual}.  For detailed configuration@comma{} see @ref{Voice Lock}.  For historical
context on Aural CSS@comma{} see
@ref{Audio Formatting And Aural CSS,,,emacsvox-heritage,Emacspeak Heritage}.

