see @ref{Forms Mode,,,emacs,The GNU Emacs Manual} for setup and operation@comma{} and
@ref{emacsvox-forms,,,emacsvox-reference,Emacsvox Reference Manual} for the
speech integration.

@subsection Experimental OCR Front End

@cindex OCR, experimental

@findex emacsvox-ocr

Module @code{emacsvox-ocr} is an experimental legacy front end@comma{} not a supported
initial-user route.  Its scanner@comma{} image-processing@comma{} and OCR-engine workflow has
not yet received a current integration review.  Do not rely on setup commands@comma{}
program paths@comma{} or defaults in the historical survey.  If you evaluate the
module@comma{} inspect its live command and option help first; the generated
@ref{emacsvox-ocr,,,emacsvox-reference,Emacsvox Reference Manual} is an API
inventory@comma{} not a validated deployment guide.  Report the exact platform@comma{}
scanner backend@comma{} OCR engine@comma{} and Emacsvox revision when requesting help.

@node Notmuch Mail
@chapter Notmuch Mail

@cindex Notmuch mail

@cindex mail search, Notmuch

@cindex asynchronous search, Notmuch

@findex notmuch

Notmuch is a separately installed mail index and Emacs interface.  Emacsvox
adds speech and semantic presentation to its Hello@comma{} Search@comma{} and Show views; it
does not configure a mail store@comma{} build the Notmuch database@comma{} fetch or send
mail@comma{} or replace Notmuch's commands.  Install compatible Notmuch command-line
and Emacs components and complete their setup first.  The
@uref{https://notmuchmail.org/doc/latest/notmuch-emacs.html, official Notmuch Emacs manual} covers that setup and the complete upstream command set.

The generated
@ref{emacsvox-notmuch,,,emacsvox-reference,Emacsvox Reference Manual} section
is the exhaustive Emacsvox command and option reference.  This chapter follows
the tasks a user performs and explains when automatic feedback occurs.

@section Start Notmuch

@cindex starting Notmuch

@cindex Notmuch Hello

@findex notmuch-search

Run @kbd{M-x notmuch} to open Notmuch Hello.  With the standard Emacsvox global
maps loaded@comma{} @kbd{C-' m} runs @code{notmuch-search} directly and prompts for a
query.  Emacsvox loads its live integration when Notmuch loads and marks new
Hello@comma{} Search@comma{} Show@comma{} and Tree buffers with Notmuch semantic context.

Notmuch remains the authority for every database@comma{} tag@comma{} archive@comma{} attachment@comma{}
and message-composition operation.  Emacsvox observes the result of an
explicit upstream command and presents it; loading the integration by itself
does not alter mail@comma{} tags@comma{} files@comma{} or Notmuch configuration.

@section Use Hello and start a search

@cindex saved searches, Notmuch

@cindex widgets, Notmuch Hello

Moving among Hello widgets speaks a saved-search label and its displayed
message count@comma{} or identifies the button or link reached.  These are the common
Hello keys in the current Notmuch map:

@table @kbd
@item TAB
Run @code{widget-forward} to reach the next saved search@comma{} entry field@comma{} button@comma{}
or link.
@item <backtab>
Run @code{widget-backward} to reach the preceding widget.
@item RET
Run @code{widget-button-press}; on a saved search this starts its query.
@item j
Run @code{notmuch-jump-search} and choose a configured search.
@item g
Run @code{notmuch-refresh-this-buffer} to refresh Hello.
@item m
Run @code{notmuch-mua-new-mail}.  Composition then belongs to Message mode and
the separate @code{emacsvox-message} integration.
@end table

Press @kbd{?} in any Notmuch view@comma{} or @kbd{C-h m}@comma{} for the complete bindings
provided by the installed Notmuch version.

@subsection Understand asynchronous search completion

@cindex search results, asynchronous rendering

@cindex notification stream, Notmuch

@vindex emacsvox-notmuch-search-completion-style

A Notmuch search buffer can appear before its rows have finished rendering.
For a user-owned non-refresh search that remains selected@comma{} Emacsvox gives a
progress cue and waits for Notmuch's process sentinel.  It does not announce an
empty row or move point while the process is still producing results.

The default @code{adaptive} completion policy distinguishes two interactions:

@itemize
@item
If the search is still selected@comma{} no command has been issued in it@comma{} and no input
is pending@comma{} completion uses the primary speech stream.  It reports the final
thread count followed by the result at point@comma{} or an explicit zero-thread
summary.

@item
If you navigate@comma{} invoke another command@comma{} leave the buffer@comma{} or refresh an
existing search before it completes@comma{} the eventual completion is a generic
count on the notification stream.  It does not reread the row you reached and
does not disclose the query@comma{} sender@comma{} recipients@comma{} subject@comma{} tags@comma{} or attachment
metadata.  Killing the owning search buffer cancels this delivery silently.
@end itemize

The notification stream uses @code{tts-notification-device} when one is
available and otherwise falls back to the primary process.  The
@code{summary} policy always sends a generic count there; @code{cue} sends only
a completion cue; and @code{silent} suppresses successful completion.
Failures remain audible and generic for every policy.  A normal refresh is
always treated as background-style completion@comma{} while the deliberate global
refresh-all command remains silent.

@section Navigate and inspect Search results

@cindex Notmuch Search

@cindex search fields, Notmuch

On thread movement@comma{} Emacsvox reads the configured semantic fields and presents
status cues.  Reaching a boundary reports the beginning or end instead of
repeating the current row.  Emacsvox makes the arrow keys equivalent to the
standard next and previous thread commands.

@table @kbd
@item n
Run @code{notmuch-search-next-thread}.
@item <down>
Also run @code{notmuch-search-next-thread}.
@item p
Run @code{notmuch-search-previous-thread}.
@item <up>
Also run @code{notmuch-search-previous-thread}.
@item RET
Run @code{notmuch-search-show-thread}.  The Show view lands on the first useful
visible body line or leaf MIME part and presents the opened message.
@item SPC
Run @code{notmuch-search-scroll-up}.  This is upstream viewport scrolling
rather than semantic thread selection.
@item C-c C-p
Run @code{emacsvox-notmuch-speak-search-details}.  This explicitly reads all
configured fields for the result at point without the automatic content
limits.
@item +
Run @code{notmuch-search-add-tag}.
@item -
Run @code{notmuch-search-remove-tag}.
@item *
Run @code{notmuch-search-tag-all} for the selected results.
@item a
Run @code{notmuch-search-archive-thread}.
@item g
Run @code{notmuch-refresh-this-buffer}; completion is delivered through the
generic notification policy described above.
@end table

The default Search fields are authors@comma{} subject@comma{} relative date@comma{} matched and
total message counts@comma{} and ordinary tags.  Status tags configured as auditory
cues are not repeated as words.  See @ref{Notmuch fields and privacy}
to omit or reorder potentially sensitive fields.

@section Read a thread and its MIME parts

@cindex Notmuch Show

@cindex MIME parts, Notmuch

@cindex attachments, Notmuch

Opening a thread speaks one semantic message summary and the first visible body
line.  Hidden alternative bodies are skipped.  If a message has no visible
body text@comma{} point falls back to a leaf MIME-part button when possible.  Moving
onto such a button identifies its bounded filename@comma{} content type@comma{} and size.

@table @kbd
@item n
Run @code{notmuch-show-next-open-message}.
@item p
Run @code{notmuch-show-previous-open-message}.
@item N
Run @code{notmuch-show-next-message}@comma{} including closed messages.
@item P
Run @code{notmuch-show-previous-message}@comma{} including closed messages.
@item SPC
Run @code{notmuch-show-advance-and-archive}.  Within one message Emacsvox reads
the newly visible page; on a message transition it reads the new message; at
the thread end it reports the actual archive outcome and destination.
@item DEL
Run @code{notmuch-show-rewind} and read the resulting page or message.
@item RET
Run @code{notmuch-show-toggle-message} and cue the current message's resulting
visibility.
@item M-RET
Run @code{notmuch-show-open-or-close-all}.  It opens every message and reports
the resulting thread state and total@comma{} such as ``Opened all 5 messages.''  Use a
prefix argument@comma{} @kbd{C-u M-RET}@comma{} to close every message and receive the
corresponding total.
@item C-c C-p
Run @code{emacsvox-notmuch-speak-show-position}.  It reports the message
ordinal and total plus complete configured message details.  On a MIME-part
button it instead gives the complete part description@comma{} including its full
filename.
@item TAB
Run @code{notmuch-show-next-button} and identify the button or MIME part.
@item <backtab>
Run @code{notmuch-show-previous-button} and identify the button or MIME part.
@item w
Run @code{notmuch-show-save-attachments}.  Emacsvox confirms when the upstream
command returns@comma{} but Notmuch does not expose a per-attachment result through
this command@comma{} so the confirmation cannot distinguish partial skips.
@item . s
Run @code{notmuch-show-save-part} on the current MIME part.
@item . v
Run @code{notmuch-show-view-part}.
@item . o
Run @code{notmuch-show-interactively-view-part} and choose an external action.
@item . m
Run @code{notmuch-show-choose-mime-of-part}.
@end table

Viewing@comma{} saving@comma{} overwriting@comma{} or launching a MIME part remains an explicit
Notmuch operation.  Merely navigating or requesting details never opens an
attachment or external viewer.

@section Change tags and archive state

@cindex tags, Notmuch

@cindex archiving, Notmuch

Emacsvox compares displayed tags before and after interactive mutation.  It
reports only the actual additions and removals.  Adding an existing tag or
removing an absent one reports ``Tags unchanged'' rather than claiming a
change.  An error or cancelled upstream command does not produce success
feedback.

The following common Show commands receive that result-aware feedback:

@table @kbd
@item +
Run @code{notmuch-show-add-tag} on the current message.
@item -
Run @code{notmuch-show-remove-tag}.
@item *
Run @code{notmuch-show-tag-all} across the thread.
@item a
Run @code{notmuch-show-archive-message-then-next-or-next-thread}.
@item A
Run @code{notmuch-show-archive-thread-then-next}.
@end table

Archive feedback distinguishes a real change@comma{} unchanged archive tags@comma{} and an
unset @code{notmuch-archive-tags} configuration.  Movement wrappers give one
combined result rather than duplicating feedback from their nested tag or
archive command.

@anchor{Notmuch fields and privacy}

@section Choose fields@comma{} status@comma{} and privacy

@cindex privacy, Notmuch speech

@cindex fields, Notmuch speech

@cindex status cues, Notmuch

@cindex automatic speech limits, Notmuch

Run @kbd{M-x customize-group RET emacsvox-notmuch RET} to change Notmuch speech
without altering Notmuch's own database or display.

@table @code
@item emacsvox-notmuch-search-result-fields
Defaults to @code{(authors subject date count tags)}.  Remove @code{subject} or
another field to keep it out of Search navigation speech@comma{} reorder fields@comma{} or
add a formatter function.
@item emacsvox-notmuch-show-message-fields
Defaults to @code{(from date to cc tags attachments)}.  The subject is omitted
by default in Show@comma{} while To and Cc recipients are included.  Remove
@code{to}@comma{} @code{cc}@comma{} or any other field when recipient privacy matters.
@item emacsvox-notmuch-search-field-separator
@itemx emacsvox-notmuch-show-field-separator
Control the text placed between configured fields; both default to comma and
space.
@item emacsvox-notmuch-search-status-icons
@itemx emacsvox-notmuch-show-status-icons
Map unread@comma{} replied@comma{} forwarded@comma{} flagged@comma{} and personal status tags to cues or
silence.  Removing an entry makes that status available to ordinary tag
speech.  The optional Aural Presentation feature
@code{mail-message-status-cues} can own shared semantic mail-state cues.
@item emacsvox-notmuch-search-completion-style
Choose @code{adaptive}@comma{} @code{summary}@comma{} @code{cue}@comma{} or @code{silent} as
described under asynchronous completion.
@end table

Mail-controlled text is bounded before automatic speech and Aural history.
The defaults are 256 characters and 1@comma{}024 UTF-8 bytes per field@comma{} then 1@comma{}000
characters and 4@comma{}096 bytes for the complete automatic presentation:

@table @code
@item emacsvox-notmuch-automatic-field-character-limit
@itemx emacsvox-notmuch-automatic-field-byte-limit
Bound each automatic field before formatting and submission.
@item emacsvox-notmuch-automatic-total-character-limit
@itemx emacsvox-notmuch-automatic-total-byte-limit
Provide the final defense for one automatic Notmuch presentation.
@item emacsvox-notmuch-mime-node-limit
@itemx emacsvox-notmuch-mime-depth-limit
Default to 4@comma{}096 cons nodes and depth 64.  They keep malformed@comma{} cyclic@comma{} broad@comma{}
or deeply nested MIME metadata from exhausting automatic inspection.  An
incomplete scan reports a conservative attachment lower bound instead of
claiming that no attachment exists.
@end table

A shortened Search or Show presentation states what was omitted and points to
@kbd{C-c C-p} for explicit full configured details; a shortened Hello item
points to @key{RET}.  The explicit Search and Show inspection commands
deliberately bypass the automatic content limits; use them only when speaking
the complete configured fields is appropriate.

@subsection Understand retained and notification content

@cindex Aural history, mail privacy

Primary-stream Notmuch presentations can enter bounded Aural Presentation
history.  By default Emacsvox retains at most 20 presentations and at most
4@comma{}096 UTF-8 bytes of speech preview per record; records contain frozen data@comma{}
source names@comma{} and positions@comma{} but never retain source buffers.  Run
@kbd{M-x emacsvox-aural-list-recent-feedback} to inspect what was retained@comma{} or
set @code{emacsvox-aural-presentation-history-limit} to zero to disable that
history.

Generic asynchronous completion uses the notification stream and contains no
query or message metadata@comma{} but notification text may be retained by the
notification subsystem.  Choose @code{cue} or @code{silent} when even a generic
thread count is unwanted@comma{} and review the global notification-device and log
settings separately.

@section Know the support boundaries

@cindex Notmuch Tree support

@cindex message composition, Notmuch

@table @asis
@item Hello
Widget navigation@comma{} view lifecycle@comma{} and searches have Notmuch-specific semantic
feedback.
@item Search
Result navigation@comma{} asynchronous lifecycle@comma{} tags@comma{} archive operations@comma{} opening@comma{}
and configured detail inspection have Notmuch-specific feedback.
@item Show
Message and part navigation@comma{} page reading@comma{} visibility@comma{} tags@comma{} archive operations@comma{}
and explicit detail inspection have Notmuch-specific feedback.
@item Tree
Tree buffers receive @code{emacsvox-aural-module} context only.  Generic core
speech can still operate@comma{} but this module currently has no Tree-specific row
formatter@comma{} navigation@comma{} mutation@comma{} visibility@comma{} or asynchronous lifecycle.
@item Composition
Notmuch uses standard Message-mode composition.  Speech support after
@code{notmuch-mua-new-mail}@comma{} reply@comma{} forward@comma{} or resume transitions belongs to
@code{emacsvox-message}@comma{} not to the Notmuch integration.
@end table

There is no independent Notmuch speech disable or unload command.  Advice@comma{}
hooks@comma{} and the Search arrow bindings remain installed for the Emacs session
once the module loads.  Repeated loading is idempotent and does not duplicate
them.

@section Troubleshoot the live integration

@cindex troubleshooting, Notmuch

@cindex byte-code, Notmuch integration

Use @kbd{?} or @kbd{C-h m} in the affected Notmuch buffer to confirm the keys
provided by the installed Notmuch version.  Run @kbd{M-x notmuch-version} to
identify that package@comma{} and use the official manual linked at the beginning of
this chapter for its current behavior.

Run @kbd{M-x emacsvox-aural-doctor} when speech bindings@comma{} loaded source versus
byte-code@comma{} Aural rules@comma{} sound resources@comma{} or the backend appear wrong.  Use
@kbd{M-x emacsvox-aural-list-recent-feedback} to inspect the exact bounded
presentation submitted for a Notmuch action.  These diagnostics do not query
or modify the mail database.

For a source checkout@comma{} use the Emacs selected by @code{local.mk}.  Run
@code{make check-emacs} before diagnosing compilation@comma{} @code{make bytecode}
after an ordinary integration edit@comma{} and @code{make bytecode-rebuild} after a
branch switch or a macro or public-interface change.  A fresh Emacs must load
the rebuilt byte-code before live verification.

@section Integration contracts for maintainers

@cindex Notmuch integration maintenance

The live module is registered as an optional package integration.  Its stable
extension points are the Hello@comma{} Search@comma{} Show@comma{} and Tree mode hooks; Search and
Show mode maps; public interactive commands; and public Search/Show accessors.
Advice is installed only when a target exists and is target-aware@comma{} so a nested
or programmatic upstream call does not claim the outer user's feedback.

The Show landing and visibility paths also read renderer metadata such as
@code{:headers-overlay}@comma{} @code{:message-visible}@comma{} message extents@comma{}
@code{:notmuch-part}@comma{} part-hidden state@comma{} and Notmuch part-button types.  Missing
optional metadata should reduce presentation detail rather than authorize an
operation.  Review these adapters whenever the Notmuch renderer changes.

Each user-owned asynchronous Search process carries its own completion state
as a process property.  The upstream sentinel renders first; Emacsvox then
clears that property before delivering exactly one terminal result.  The
owning buffer and process status are authoritative.  A killed owner remains
silent@comma{} concurrent buffers cannot overwrite one another@comma{} and the global
refresh-all path intentionally acquires no ownership.

Run @code{make notmuch-test} for the explicit source suite.  After current
byte-code exists@comma{} run @code{make compiled-notmuch-test}; it verifies the exact
loaded @code{emacsvox-notmuch.elc}@comma{} compiler version@comma{} and the same focused
selector@comma{} including real displayed-window reading for both advance paths.

@node Agent Shell
@chapter Agent Shell

@cindex Agent Shell

@cindex artificial intelligence agents

Agent Shell is a separately installed Emacs package for interactive AI-agent
sessions.  Emacsvox adds speech and semantic presentation without replacing
Agent Shell's provider setup@comma{} session management@comma{} prompt editor@comma{} or permission
controls.  See @uref{https://github.com/xenodium/agent-shell, Agent Shell} for
provider installation and configuration.

The generated
@ref{emacsvox-agent-shell,,,emacsvox-reference,Emacsvox Reference Manual}
section is the exhaustive Emacsvox command and option reference.  This chapter
follows the tasks a user performs in a session.

@section Start and control a session

Load Emacsvox and start Agent Shell with @code{M-x agent-shell}.  Emacsvox loads
its integration after Agent Shell and activates both new and already existing
shell buffers.  Run @code{M-x emacsvox-agent-shell-enable} only when manually
re-enabling support after it was disabled or while diagnosing a live reload.

Compose and submit prompts with Agent Shell's normal editing commands.  The
live prompt retains Emacs undo.  Submitted transcript text is history rather
than editable prompt input.  Press @kbd{C-c C-c} to ask Agent Shell to interrupt
an active request; its normal confirmation setting still applies.

Emacsvox speaks routine content only at complete turn boundaries.  A network
pause does not make a partial answer sound complete.  A cancelled@comma{} refused@comma{} or
failed turn reports its outcome without replacing the last successfully
completed response.

@section Choose automatic speech and privacy

The focused session defaults to @code{response}: completed answers and routine
completion feedback are presented@comma{} while thought@comma{} plan@comma{} and tool chatter is
suppressed.  An unfocused session defaults to @code{notify}: its notification
names the session but does not disclose response or tool content.

@table @code
@item full
Speak responses plus configured thought@comma{} plan@comma{} and tool detail.  Full tool
output can contain commands@comma{} file contents@comma{} or other sensitive provider data.
@item response
Speak completed agent responses while suppressing routine thought@comma{} plan@comma{} and
tool content.
@item notify
Present routine completion without reading the response body.
@item quiet
Suppress routine automatic feedback.  Explicit inspection remains available.
@item auto
Use @code{emacsvox-agent-shell-foreground-speech-level} or
@code{emacsvox-agent-shell-background-speech-level} according to focus.
@end table

Press @kbd{C-c C-q} to run
@code{emacsvox-agent-shell-select-speech-level} and choose the current
session's level@comma{} including @code{auto}.  Press @kbd{C-c C-S-q} to run
@code{emacsvox-agent-shell-select-background-speech-level} and change the
shared background default.
@code{M-x emacsvox-agent-shell-cycle-speech-level} cycles toward less routine
speech; use a prefix argument to restore @code{auto}.

Thought content defaults to an icon under
@code{emacsvox-agent-shell-speak-thought-process}.  Tool feedback is separately
controlled by @code{emacsvox-agent-shell-speak-tool-calls} and
@code{emacsvox-agent-shell-tool-output-verbosity}.  Selecting background
@code{response} or @code{full}@comma{} thought @code{speak}@comma{} or full tool output is an
explicit disclosure choice.

Automatic response@comma{} tool@comma{} error@comma{} permission@comma{} and similar text is limited by
@code{emacsvox-agent-shell-automatic-content-max-characters}@comma{} which defaults to
2@comma{}000 characters.  A shortened presentation gives exact omitted character and
line counts and tells you where to inspect the full content.  The complete text
stays in the Agent Shell buffer; the limit is applied before Aural compiles the
automatic speech copy.

@section Respond to urgent permissions

Permission requests are separate from routine speech levels because they can
block the agent and require a decision.  With
@code{emacsvox-agent-shell-speak-permissions} enabled@comma{} a request interrupts the
appropriate speech stream@comma{} identifies the session and requested operation@comma{} and
announces its choices.  Provider-controlled descriptions and choice labels are
bounded@comma{} but the actual permission controls remain in Agent Shell.

If the request belongs to another session@comma{} switch to the named buffer.  Use
@code{M-x agent-shell-jump-to-latest-permission-button-row} to find its latest
permission row@comma{} or use Agent Shell's @kbd{TAB} and @kbd{S-TAB} item navigation.
Move among the buttons and press @kbd{RET}@comma{} or use a direct choice key displayed
by Agent Shell.  Choice keys are supplied by Agent Shell and the provider@comma{} so
the visible permission row is authoritative.

Error blocks remain exceptional feedback rather than routine transcript
chatter.  Lifecycle error announcements follow
@code{emacsvox-agent-shell-signal-processing}; rendered error content remains
available in the transcript even when routine response speech is quiet.

@section Inspect a response on demand

These commands work from the shell and its associated viewports:

@table @kbd
@item C-c r
Run @code{emacsvox-agent-shell-speak-last-response}.  It reads the latest
successfully completed response in full without moving point.  This deliberate
operation does not inherit the automatic 2@comma{}000-character limit.  While a new
turn is busy it explicitly identifies and reads the prior completed response.
@item C-c R
Run @code{emacsvox-agent-shell-speak-response-overview}.  It reports line@comma{}
heading@comma{} source-block@comma{} and table counts plus a bounded opening preview.
@end table

These commands remain usable at @code{notify} and @code{quiet}.  If no turn has
completed@comma{} they say that no completed response is available rather than
presenting a streaming partial or failed answer.

@section Navigate transcript blocks

Agent Shell responses contain semantic prompt@comma{} response@comma{} thought@comma{} activity@comma{}
tool@comma{} plan@comma{} permission@comma{} error@comma{} source-block@comma{} and table locations.  Emacsvox
navigation reports the block type@comma{} visibility@comma{} and a bounded body preview.

@table @kbd
@item ]
Run @code{emacsvox-agent-shell-next-block-at-point} to find the next block of
the current semantic type.
@item [
Run @code{emacsvox-agent-shell-previous-block-at-point} to find the preceding
block of the current semantic type.
@item C-c ]
Run @code{emacsvox-agent-shell-next-block-of-type}@comma{} choose a type@comma{} and move to
its next occurrence.
@item C-c [
Run @code{emacsvox-agent-shell-previous-block-of-type}@comma{} choose a type@comma{} and move
to its preceding occurrence.
@end table

After a successful typed jump@comma{} @kbd{]} and @kbd{[} repeat in that direction.
At an editable live prompt@comma{} bare brackets insert themselves instead of taking
over prompt editing.

@section Read and copy source blocks

Use typed block navigation to reach a source block@comma{} then:

@table @kbd
@item C-c C-b
Run @code{emacsvox-agent-shell-speak-source-block} to read the complete rendered
source block with its language and line count.
@item C-c C-y
Run @code{emacsvox-agent-shell-copy-source-block} to copy the logical source
through Agent Shell's public copy operation.
@end table

The source commands are explicit operations and do not use the automatic
content limit.  Ordinary line navigation retains its separate bounded speech
policy.

@section Navigate and copy rendered tables

Moving into a rendered Markdown table activates a contextual table map.  It
announces the table dimensions and current cell.  Leaving the table restores
the ordinary Agent Shell keys.

@table @kbd
@item <right>
Run @code{emacsvox-agent-shell-table-next-column}.
@item <left>
Run @code{emacsvox-agent-shell-table-previous-column}.
@item <down>
Run @code{emacsvox-agent-shell-table-next-row}.
@item <up>
Run @code{emacsvox-agent-shell-table-previous-row}.
@item M-<down>
Run @code{emacsvox-agent-shell-table-exit-forward}.
@item M-<up>
Run @code{emacsvox-agent-shell-table-exit-backward}.
@item SPC
Run @code{emacsvox-agent-shell-table-speak-cell}.
@item r
Run @code{emacsvox-agent-shell-table-speak-row}.
@item c
Run @code{emacsvox-agent-shell-table-speak-column}.
@item .
Run @code{emacsvox-agent-shell-table-speak-context} for row@comma{} column@comma{} and table
position.
@item =
Run @code{emacsvox-agent-shell-table-speak-dimensions}.
@item w
Run @code{emacsvox-agent-shell-table-copy-cell}.
@item k k
Also run @code{emacsvox-agent-shell-table-copy-cell}.
@item k r
Run @code{emacsvox-agent-shell-table-copy-row}; copied cells are tab-separated.
@item k c
Run @code{emacsvox-agent-shell-table-copy-column}; copied cells are separated by
newlines.
@item a
Run @code{emacsvox-agent-shell-table-select-speaking-method} to toggle column
titles@comma{} row titles@comma{} or whether data is spoken before titles.
@end table

Copy commands use logical cell values@comma{} excluding renderer padding and Markdown
borders.  Wrapped cell contents remain complete.

@section Recover from interruption or failure

If a request is taking the wrong path@comma{} press @kbd{C-c C-c} and confirm the
Agent Shell interrupt.  A failed or interrupted partial response is not cached
as complete.  Press @kbd{C-c r} for the preceding completed response@comma{} or
@kbd{C-c R} for its overview.  Read the error block in the transcript for the
provider's details and edit the live prompt before resubmitting.

Use @code{M-x agent-shell-restart} when Agent Shell itself needs a fresh process
for the same project.  Use @code{M-x emacsvox-agent-shell-enable} to reinstall
speech hooks after deliberately disabling them or during live integration
development.  Neither recovery command discards transcript files or project
work.

@section Integration ownership for maintainers

The integration uses three deliberately different contract layers:

@multitable @columnfractions .18 .37 .45
@headitem Layer
@tab Agent Shell owner
@tab Emacsvox rule
@item Public structured API
@tab @code{agent-shell-subscribe-to}@comma{} @code{agent-shell-unsubscribe}@comma{} @code{agent-shell-status}@comma{} and @code{agent-shell-shell-buffer}; lifecycle@comma{} permission@comma{} and tool events
@tab Primary authority for processing@comma{} completion@comma{} errors@comma{} permission urgency@comma{} and tool status. The public turn boundary triggers inspection of renderer-owned response sections. Event and response data is treated as untrusted content and bounded before automatic Aural submission.
@item Experimental renderer metadata
@tab @code{agent-shell-ui-state}@comma{} @code{agent-shell-ui-section}@comma{} rendered Markdown table/source properties@comma{} and renderer-owned faces
@tab Feature-detected navigation and presentation input. Renderer group provenance outranks provider-controlled IDs; prose and IDs cannot manufacture structured permission or lifecycle authority.
@item Private compatibility fallback
@tab The aggregate @code{agent-shell--state}@comma{} the legacy viewport ownership resolver@comma{} viewport position helpers@comma{} and legacy face@comma{} prompt@comma{} or ID inference
@tab Kept in narrow adapter functions for older Agent Shell releases or incomplete renderers. A missing optional detail may reduce header or navigation richness but must not create a permission action or completed response.
@end multitable

The lifecycle subscription currently consumes @code{init-started}@comma{}
@code{init-finished}@comma{} @code{session-selected}@comma{} @code{session-restored}@comma{}
@code{input-submitted}@comma{} @code{turn-complete}@comma{} and @code{error}.  Dedicated
subscriptions consume @code{permission-request}@comma{} @code{permission-response}@comma{}
and @code{tool-call-update}.  Add new semantic behavior at the public event
layer when Agent Shell exposes it; keep renderer and private fallbacks isolated
and covered by compatibility tests.

@node EAT Terminal Access
@chapter Speech Access To EAT

@cindex EAT terminal emulator

@cindex terminal accessibility

@cindex speech access, terminal

@findex eat

EAT (Emulate A Terminal) is a third-party terminal emulator; it is not part of
stock Emacs.  It is commonly installed from NonGNU ELPA@.  Start it with
@kbd{M-x eat}; use a prefix argument to create or select another EAT terminal.
See the EAT manual for terminal setup and EAT's complete command reference
(@pxref{Top,,,eat,Eat User Manual}).

Emacsvox observes EAT's bounded rendered screen.  It does not parse a shell's
private state and it does not generate shell or router completion candidates.
This distinction is important: EAT handles terminal emulation@comma{} the program on
the other end handles terminal-side input and completion@comma{} and Emacsvox
presents the visible result.

@section Input Modes And Completion Responsibilities

@cindex EAT input modes

@cindex completion, EAT

@cindex Corfu, EAT

@findex eat-semi-char-mode

@findex eat-char-mode

@findex eat-emacs-mode

@findex eat-line-mode

@kindex C-c C-j (EAT)

@kindex C-c M-d (EAT)

@kindex C-c C-e (EAT)

@kindex C-c C-l (EAT)

EAT provides four input modes.  Emacsvox announces a human-readable mode name
whenever you switch modes.

@table @asis
@item Semi-character mode
This is EAT's default.  Most keys@comma{} including @key{TAB}@comma{} go to the terminal@comma{}
while Emacs prefixes such as @kbd{C-x}@comma{} @kbd{C-c}@comma{} and @kbd{M-x} remain
available.  Use @kbd{C-q} before a normally reserved key to send it to the
terminal.  Switch here with @kbd{C-c C-j}.
@item Character mode
All supported keys go to the terminal@comma{} which is useful for applications that
need Emacs-like keys.  Switch here with @kbd{C-c M-d}; return to semi-character
mode with @kbd{C-M-m} or @kbd{M-@key{RET}}.
@item Emacs mode
Keys remain in Emacs and no input is sent to the terminal.  Use this for normal
Emacs buffer motion and inspection.  Switch here with @kbd{C-c C-e}.
@item Line mode
The pending input line is edited locally and sent on @key{RET}.  @key{TAB} runs
Emacs's @code{completion-at-point}@comma{} so a configured Emacs completion front end
such as Corfu can display its result.  Switch here from semi-character or Emacs
mode with @kbd{C-c C-l}.  Line mode is not available in EAT's embedded Eshell
terminal.
@end table

In semi-character and character mode@comma{} @key{TAB} belongs to Bash Readline@comma{} a
remote shell@comma{} a router CLI@comma{} or whichever terminal application is running.
Corfu is not involved.  In line mode@comma{} @key{TAB} belongs to Emacs completion and
does not ask a remote CLI for candidates.  Therefore semi-character mode is
usually the right choice for SSH sessions and network-device CLIs.  If an
Emacs completion backend blocks in line mode@comma{} @kbd{C-g} cancels that Emacs
operation; switch to semi-character mode if terminal-side completion was
intended.

Shell completion must itself be configured.  For example@comma{} Bash can complete
@code{git pul} to @code{git pull} only when the shell has suitable programmable
completion definitions@comma{} often supplied by a system @code{bash-completion}
package.  Emacsvox observes the result but neither installs nor substitutes for
those definitions.

@section Automatic Terminal Feedback

@cindex EAT automatic speech

@cindex terminal output, speech policy

Automatic speech is restricted to the selected EAT buffer while its selected
window follows the live terminal cursor.  Moving into scrollback prevents new
output from interrupting review.  Output in another terminal or behind an
active minibuffer is silent by default.

On the main screen@comma{} Emacsvox coalesces a burst before classifying it.  Complete
new output rows are spoken once@comma{} with an automatic limit of eight rows and
1@comma{}000 characters.  Continued input rows and empty terminal placeholders are
omitted without hiding the output or replaying earlier screen rows.  The
redrawn prompt is not repeated.  Carriage-return progress and status rewrites
use rate-limited replaceable speech.  Unchanged repaint and resize-only updates
remain silent.  Terminal bells@comma{} paste actions@comma{} input-mode changes@comma{}
application-screen boundaries@comma{} and process exit are content-free semantic
events rather than arbitrary screen speech.

In semi-character and character mode on the main screen@comma{} @key{RET} is silent.
After a rendered Left or Right movement@comma{} Emacsvox names the character reached;
after Up or Down@comma{} it speaks the resulting input or history row without the
observed shell prompt.  @kbd{M-b}@comma{} @kbd{M-f}@comma{} and modified Left or Right word
movement speak the exact rendered text crossed.  A boundary key that produces
no movement stays silent.

An echoed Space is named without reading the prompt or the rest of the line.
For @key{BACKSPACE}@comma{} @key{DELETE}@comma{} @kbd{M-d}@comma{} and @kbd{C-k}@comma{} Emacsvox compares
bounded public screen snapshots before and after the key.  When one same-row
removal is unambiguous@comma{} it speaks the character or text actually removed.
Otherwise it gives only a deletion tone; it never guesses from adjacent prompt
or screen text.  Terminal padding and trailing input blanks do not prevent an
otherwise unambiguous word deletion from being announced.  This also handles a
no-echo peer safely: without a visible deletion there is no content to
announce.  Feedback requires the selected window to follow the live cursor@comma{}
and is silent during protected password input@comma{} in an unselected terminal@comma{} and
on an alternate screen where the application controls the keys.

On a full-screen or alternate-screen application@comma{} Emacsvox does not read every
refresh.  A cursor row reached by correlated arrow-style navigation@comma{} or one
compact high-confidence transfer of selection-like styling@comma{} can be announced.
Ambiguous repaint remains silent and available through explicit review.  In
particular@comma{} inverse video or a colored row alone is not treated as proof of the
active item.

@section Terminal-side Completion

@cindex completion candidates, terminal

@cindex router completion

@kindex TAB (EAT terminal completion)

On the main terminal screen@comma{} Emacsvox takes a public screen snapshot before a
terminal-side @key{TAB} and waits briefly for EAT output to settle.

@itemize
@item
A unique inline completion speaks the newly displayed field.  For example@comma{}
@code{~/sr<TAB>} can speak @code{src slash} after the shell displays
@code{~/src/}.

@item
An ambiguous completion that prints columns or help rows is presented as one
candidate transaction after the input line is redrawn.  Small inferred lists
speak up to eight candidates.  Descriptive router-style rows retain their
layout instead of being forced into a shell-token model.

@item
Large lists are counted and truncated for automatic speech.  If candidates
have already scrolled beyond the bounded visible screen@comma{} Emacsvox reports an
honest lower bound such as ``at least N visible'' rather than inventing a total.

@item
Repeated identical lists@comma{} split output chunks@comma{} terminal bells@comma{} wrapping@comma{} and
screen resize are coalesced without replaying the same candidate text.
@end itemize

The completion result is derived from the rendered screen@comma{} so the same design
works with local Readline@comma{} SSH@comma{} and unstructured router help.  If the peer makes
no visible change@comma{} there is nothing reliable to announce.  On an alternate
screen@comma{} @key{TAB} is treated as navigation rather than shell completion.

Use @kbd{C-e q c} to speak the latest retained candidate or help layout@comma{} or
open frozen review with @kbd{C-e q r} and press @kbd{c}.  These commands are
especially useful after a large or multi-column result.

@section Explicit Review Commands

@cindex EAT review commands

@cindex terminal screen review

@findex emacsvox-eat-speak-current-row

@findex emacsvox-eat-speak-last-change

@findex emacsvox-eat-speak-likely-focus

@findex emacsvox-eat-speak-completion-output

@findex emacsvox-eat-speak-visible-screen

@findex emacsvox-eat-review-screen

@findex emacsvox-eat-speak-retained-status

@findex emacsvox-eat-speak-retained-metadata

@findex emacsvox-eat-cycle-verbosity

@findex emacsvox-eat-toggle-background-monitoring

@kindex C-e q (EAT review)

The default Emacsvox prefix is @kbd{C-e}.  In every EAT input mode@comma{}
@kbd{C-e q} opens the terminal review prefix:

@table @kbd
@item C-e q l
Speak the cursor row from the latest retained screen.
@item C-e q d
Speak the last classified screen change.
@item C-e q h
Speak the latest conservative likely-focus inference and its confidence.
@item C-e q c
Speak the retained completion candidates or help rows.
@item C-e q s
Speak a bounded copy of the retained visible screen.
@item C-e q r
Open a read-only frozen copy for line-by-line review.
@item C-e q t
Speak the retained progress@comma{} application@comma{} or shell-prompt status.
@item C-e q i
Speak the retained sanitized title or working-directory change.
@item C-e q v
Cycle terse@comma{} normal@comma{} and verbose automatic feedback for this terminal.
@item C-e q m
Toggle content-free background-output monitoring for this terminal.
@end table

These commands inspect concealed-redacted retained snapshots; they do not send
terminal input.  Content is bounded when spoken.  The visible screen is
retained@comma{} not arbitrary scrollback.

@subsection Frozen Screen Review

@cindex frozen EAT review

@kbd{C-e q r} opens an immutable copy of the latest visible screen.  It does
not refresh and navigation cannot move the live terminal cursor or send input.
The copy preserves normalized terminal styling@comma{} cursor and likely-focus
anchors@comma{} and the exact retained completion rows.  Opening and closing review
play distinct object cues and announce the transition.

@table @kbd
@item n@comma{} p; C-n@comma{} C-p; down@comma{} up
Move by captured terminal row and speak the destination@comma{} interrupting the
previous row announcement when navigation continues.
@item SPC@comma{} RET@comma{} .
Speak the current captured row.
@item a
Speak a bounded form of the current screen or completion view.
@item s@comma{} c
Show captured screen rows or retained completion/help rows.
@item C
Go to the captured terminal cursor.
@item h
Go to the retained likely focus@comma{} when one exists.
@item [@comma{} ]
Move among selection-like inverse/background styled regions.  These regions
are deliberately not labelled as focus.
@item t@comma{} i
Speak status or metadata copied when review was opened.
@item v@comma{} m
Change verbosity or background monitoring in the source terminal without
sending terminal input.  The copied content itself remains frozen.
@item ?
Describe frozen review mode.
@item q
Kill the content-bearing review buffer and restore its window.
@end table

Starting secure input@comma{} resetting or restarting the terminal@comma{} killing the
source@comma{} or leaving EAT mode also destroys its frozen review.  This bounds the
lifetime of copied terminal content.

@section Verbosity And Background Monitoring

@vindex emacsvox-eat-verbosity

@vindex emacsvox-eat-monitor-background-output

@code{emacsvox-eat-verbosity} is buffer-local:

@table @code
@item terse
Retain routine output and metadata for explicit review without speaking them
automatically.  Actionable failures and content-free lifecycle feedback remain
available.
@item normal
The default.  Speak bounded command output@comma{} progress/status@comma{} completion@comma{} and
actionable failures.
@item verbose
Also speak bounded terminal title and working-directory changes and successful
shell-command status when EAT shell integration provides it.
@end table

Background monitoring is off by default.  When enabled with @kbd{C-e q m}@comma{} an
unselected terminal plays only a rate-limited content-free cue.  On returning@comma{}
Emacsvox reports only the unread burst count@comma{} never the terminal's identity or
output.  Cursor movement@comma{} style changes@comma{} repaint@comma{} resize@comma{} and process generation
changes do not count as background output.

@section Shell Integration And Command Status

@cindex EAT shell integration

@cindex shell command status

EAT's Bash or Zsh shell integration is required for prompt annotation@comma{}
directory tracking@comma{} and per-command running/success/failure status.  For Bash@comma{}
EAT documents this initialization:

@example
[ -n "$EAT_SHELL_INTEGRATION_DIR" ] && \
  source "$EAT_SHELL_INTEGRATION_DIR/bash"
@end example

For Zsh@comma{} source the @code{zsh} script instead:

@example
[ -n "$EAT_SHELL_INTEGRATION_DIR" ] && \
  source "$EAT_SHELL_INTEGRATION_DIR/zsh"
@end example

@vindex eat-enable-shell-prompt-annotation

With shell integration and @code{eat-enable-shell-prompt-annotation} enabled@comma{}
Emacsvox retains running@comma{} successful@comma{} and failed command status.  A failure is
announced automatically at every verbosity.  Success is automatic only in
verbose mode and only after observed command activity; the initial successful
prompt does not chatter.  Running state remains silent.  Use @kbd{C-e q t} for
the retained current state.

This status is not guessed from prompt characters.  A remote shell or router
normally does not provide EAT's per-command integration@comma{} so remote commands can
still have accessible screen output and completion while their individual exit
status remains unavailable.

@section Passwords And Sensitive Content

@cindex password input, EAT

@cindex terminal privacy

@findex eat-send-password

Use @kbd{M-x eat-send-password} for a terminal password.  EAT reads it with
Emacs's protected password reader instead of recording the keystrokes in
command lossage.  During this command Emacsvox suppresses terminal observation
and bell speech@comma{} clears content-bearing retained state before and after@comma{} and
announces only whether secure input was sent or cancelled.

Emacsvox intentionally does not guess that arbitrary no-echo terminal input is
a password: EAT exposes no public echo-state signal@comma{} and a quiet repaint is not
proof.  Typing a secret through ordinary terminal input can leave keystrokes in
Emacs lossage and does not establish the same privacy boundary.  Backspace or
Delete in such input can produce a content-free deletion tone@comma{} but Emacsvox
does not name text unless EAT exposes one unambiguous visible removal.

Selected foreground output can contain sensitive information and may be
spoken.  A frozen review is a real in-memory copy until it is killed@comma{} although
visually concealed cells are redacted at snapshot time.  Verbose title and
directory speech can disclose host or path metadata.  Background monitoring@comma{}
by contrast@comma{} never speaks content or terminal identity.

@section Terminal-Controlled Clipboard Trust

@cindex clipboard security, EAT

@cindex OSC 52, EAT

@cindex remote terminal trust

@vindex eat-enable-kill-from-terminal

@vindex eat-enable-yank-to-terminal

EAT supports terminal selection control sequences@comma{} commonly called OSC 52.
Consequently@comma{} trust applies not only to a local shell but also to an SSH peer@comma{}
router CLI@comma{} or any program whose output reaches EAT's terminal parser.  Even
displaying an untrusted file can pass terminal control sequences through a
program that does not sanitize them.

In the audited EAT 0.9.4 release@comma{} @code{eat-enable-kill-from-terminal} defaults
to non-@code{nil}.  Despite the potentially confusing name@comma{} this is the
terminal-to-Emacs direction: it permits a terminal program to place text in
Emacs's kill ring.  @code{eat-enable-yank-to-terminal} defaults to @code{nil}
for security.  Enabling it permits a terminal program to request the current
Emacs kill-ring text.  Verify these defaults after upgrading EAT@.

For terminals connected to an endpoint whose output is not fully trusted@comma{}
disable both directions.  They can be customized globally@comma{} or disabled only
in the current EAT buffer with:

@lisp
(setq-local eat-enable-kill-from-terminal nil
            eat-enable-yank-to-terminal nil)
@end lisp

Run that expression with @kbd{M-:} in the EAT buffer before processing
untrusted output.  Enabling kill-ring read-back should be reserved for terminal
programs and remote endpoints that are trusted with whatever Emacs currently
holds in its kill ring.

Emacsvox deliberately changes neither EAT option.  A terminal-originated
selection payload is not an accessibility command: Emacsvox does not speak its
contents or retain it as terminal screen text.  The content-free paste
announcements described elsewhere apply to explicit user paste commands@comma{} not
to terminal-originated selection control sequences.

@section Full-screen Applications And Limitations

@cindex curses applications, EAT

@cindex alternate screen, EAT

Programs such as pagers@comma{} editors@comma{} menus@comma{} and refreshing dashboards frequently
rewrite existing cells instead of printing lines.  Emacsvox uses conservative
cursor@comma{} style-change@comma{} input-direction@comma{} and timing evidence.  Arrow-key movement
and clear paired highlight movement work best.  Printable navigation keys such
as @kbd{h}@comma{} @kbd{j}@comma{} @kbd{k}@comma{} and @kbd{l} are not assumed to be navigation@comma{}
because they may instead be application text.

Use @kbd{C-e q l}@comma{} @kbd{C-e q d}@comma{} and @kbd{C-e q h} after a silent update.  For
systematic review use @kbd{C-e q r}@comma{} then navigate rows and selection-like
styled regions.  Rapid dashboards are intentionally not read on every refresh.
Hyperlink targets@comma{} sixel/image semantics@comma{} an application's true accessibility
focus@comma{} and exact update causes are not exposed by EAT's current public API@comma{} so
Emacsvox cannot infer them reliably from pixels and faces alone.

Remote programs also need terminal information compatible with EAT@.  Broken
cursor addressing or screen layout caused by missing remote terminfo cannot be
repaired by speech classification; consult EAT's manual when the visual
terminal itself renders incorrectly.

@section EAT In Eshell

@cindex Eshell, EAT accessibility

@findex eat-eshell-mode

@findex eat-eshell-visual-command-mode

When EAT's global @code{eat-eshell-mode} is enabled@comma{} Emacsvox uses the same
screen observer for the terminal region embedded in Eshell.  Rendered command
output is handled by one speech path@comma{} so it is not repeated by generic Eshell
output feedback.

With @code{eat-eshell-visual-command-mode}@comma{} an Eshell visual command runs in a
separate EAT buffer.  Emacsvox announces its start@comma{} delivers eligible final
output before the process-exit event@comma{} and labels its exit as an Eshell visual
command.  EAT and Eshell still determine the window-return policy.  In
particular@comma{} @code{eshell-destroy-buffer-when-process-dies} determines whether a
successful visual-command buffer is killed and Eshell is restored; Emacsvox
does not change that option.

@node Running Terminal Based Applications
@chapter Term Terminal Access

@cindex Term mode

@cindex terminal applications

@cindex Eterm, Emacsvox module name

Emacsvox speech-enables the built-in Emacs Term terminal emulator through the
module named @code{emacsvox-eterm}.  The module name is inherited; users do not
install a separate Eterm package.

For a new full-terminal workflow@comma{} prefer @ref{EAT Terminal Access}.  EAT has
the richer current guide for bounded screen output@comma{} terminal-side completion@comma{}
scrollback@comma{} alternate screens@comma{} and asynchronous rendering.  Use built-in Term
when avoiding an additional package matters or an application specifically
works better there.  For ordinary line-oriented shell commands@comma{} @kbd{M-x shell} is usually simpler than either terminal emulator.

@menu
* Starting Term::
* Term Input Modes::
* Term Speech Feedback::
* Reviewing The Term Screen::
* Term Help And Recovery::
@end menu

@node Starting Term
@section Starting Term

@findex term

Run @kbd{M-x term @key{RET}}.  At the @code{Run program:} prompt@comma{} accept the
proposed shell or enter another executable and press @key{RET}.  Emacsvox
currently makes the selected Term window fill the frame when @code{term} or
@code{ansi-term} starts.  This changes only the window layout: it does not kill
the buffers that were displayed in the other windows.

Term handles terminal emulation@comma{} while the child program handles its prompts@comma{}
input@comma{} and completion.  Emacsvox observes the rendered Term buffer and adds
speech feedback and review commands.  See @ref{Term Mode,,,emacs,The GNU Emacs Manual} for the emulator itself and
@ref{emacsvox-eterm,,,emacsvox-reference,Emacsvox Reference Manual} for the
complete Emacsvox API inventory.

@node Term Input Modes
@section Character And Line Input Modes

@cindex character mode, Term

@cindex line mode, Term

@findex term-char-mode

@findex term-line-mode

Term starts in character mode.  Choose the mode according to who should receive
your keys:

@table @asis
@item Character mode
Most keys are sent immediately to the terminal program.  Use this for
full-screen or key-driven terminal applications.  Press @kbd{C-c C-j} to enter
line mode.  Because normal Emacs prefixes may belong to the child program@comma{} use
@kbd{C-c C-h m} for mode help and @kbd{C-c M-x} to run an Emacs command.
@item Line mode
Normal Emacs editing commands edit the pending input.  @key{RET} sends that
input to the terminal program.  Use @kbd{C-h m} and other ordinary Emacs Help
keys directly.  Press @kbd{C-c C-k} to return to character mode.
@end table

Emacsvox announces @code{Terminal line mode} or @code{Terminal character mode}
after an interactive switch.  In either mode@comma{} @kbd{C-t} begins the Emacsvox
Term review prefix.  To send a literal @kbd{C-t} to the child program in
character mode@comma{} press @kbd{C-t C-t}.

@node Term Speech Feedback
@section Automatic Speech Feedback

@cindex automatic speech, Term

@vindex emacsvox-eterm-autospeak

@findex emacsvox-toggle-eterm-autospeak

Automatic feedback is controlled by the buffer-local setting
@code{emacsvox-eterm-autospeak}@comma{} which is on by default.  Toggle it for the
current Term buffer with @kbd{C-t C-q}.  The older spelling
@code{eterm-autospeak} is not an Emacsvox variable.

The Term buffer must be displayed in a live window for output to be spoken
automatically.  A hidden Term buffer can continue running without speaking.
In line mode@comma{} Emacsvox reads the newly inserted output range without
interrupting it for every character.  In character mode@comma{} it presents rendered
cursor movement@comma{} characters@comma{} words@comma{} or the reached line according to how the
screen changed; new terminal activity can stop earlier speech.

This feedback is based on Term's rendered buffer and cursor coordinates.  It
does not provide EAT's bounded screen transactions@comma{} terminal-completion model@comma{}
or alternate-screen policy.  Use explicit review whenever automatic output is
too brief or an application redraw is ambiguous.

@node Reviewing The Term Screen
@section Reviewing The Term Screen

@cindex review pointer, Term

@cindex review mode, Term

The Emacsvox review pointer is independent of the terminal cursor@comma{} so reviewing
old screen text does not send keys or move the child application.  These core
commands keep the normal @kbd{C-t} prefix:

@table @kbd
@item C-t .
Move the review pointer to the terminal cursor and announce its coordinates.
@item C-t p
@itemx C-t n
Move the review pointer to the previous or next line and speak it.  The Up and
Down arrow keys are equivalent after the @kbd{C-t} prefix.
@item C-t @key{LEFT}
@itemx C-t @key{RIGHT}
Move the pointer by a character and speak the character reached.
@item C-t M-b
@itemx C-t M-f
Move the pointer backward or forward by a word and speak it.
@item C-t l
@itemx C-t w
@itemx C-t c
Speak the line@comma{} word@comma{} or character at the review pointer without moving it.
@item C-t @key{SPC}
Speak from the review pointer to the current end of the Term buffer.
@item C-t s
Prompt for text@comma{} search forward from the review pointer@comma{} and speak the matching
line.
@end table

In character mode@comma{} @kbd{C-t q} enters dedicated review mode.  The terminal
stops receiving ordinary keys while this review keymap is active.  Use
@kbd{p}@comma{} @kbd{n}@comma{} the arrow keys@comma{} @kbd{M-b}@comma{} @kbd{M-f}@comma{} @kbd{l}@comma{} @kbd{w}@comma{}
@kbd{c}@comma{} and @kbd{s} without the @kbd{C-t} prefix; press @kbd{q} to return to
character mode.  To copy reviewed text@comma{} press @kbd{m} at one endpoint@comma{} move the
pointer@comma{} and press @kbd{y} to copy the region to the kill ring.

The integration also supports named rectangular screen regions and focus or
filter windows.  Those are advanced@comma{} coordinate-based facilities; inspect the
live @kbd{C-h m} output and the generated module reference before using them.

@node Term Help And Recovery
@section Help And Recovery

@itemize
@item
If terminal output is visible but silent@comma{} press @kbd{C-t C-q} once and retry.
Automatic speech applies only while the Term buffer is displayed.

@item
If an Emacs command is being sent to the child program@comma{} remember that you are
probably in character mode.  Use @kbd{C-c C-h m}@comma{} @kbd{C-c M-x}@comma{} or switch to
line mode with @kbd{C-c C-j}.

@item
If review has drifted away from live output@comma{} press @kbd{C-t .}.  If dedicated
review mode controls the keys@comma{} press @kbd{q} first to return to character mode.

@item
If a full-screen application redraws in a way that Term cannot present
reliably@comma{} retry it in @ref{EAT Terminal Access} and use EAT's explicit review
commands.
@end itemize

@node Emacsvox Keyboard Commands
@chapter Emacsvox Keyboard Commands.

@cindex Emacs Keyboard Commands

This chapter explains how to discover the bindings that are current in
your Emacs session.  For the small starter set@comma{} see @ref{Basic Usage}.
For the complete generated default bindings@comma{} see
@ref{Emacsvox Keymaps,,,emacsvox-reference,Emacsvox Reference Manual}.

Most Emacsvox commands use one of these two default prefixes:

@table @kbd
@item C-e
The main Emacsvox keymap.
@item C-e d
The text-to-speech keymap.
@end table

Emacsvox also defines integration-specific and optional convenience
maps.  Which of those keys are available depends on the current major
and minor modes@comma{} your window system or terminal@comma{} and your configuration;
therefore this conceptual chapter does not duplicate their inventories.

Use Emacs Help to inspect the effective result:

@itemize
@item
@kbd{C-h m} describes the modes and their bindings in the current buffer.

@item
@kbd{C-h k}@comma{} followed by a key@comma{} describes the command that key invokes.

@item
After typing a prefix such as @kbd{C-e}@comma{} type @kbd{C-h} to display that
prefix's current bindings.
@end itemize

If live Help and this manual differ@comma{} live Help describes your running
session.  Check customizations first@comma{} then compare the generated defaults
in the @ref{Emacsvox Keymaps,,,emacsvox-reference,Emacsvox Reference Manual}.

@node TTS Servers
@chapter Speech Server Protocols

Emacsvox produces spoken output by communicating with one of several speech
servers.  Two protocol families coexist:

@itemize
@item
The legacy command-and-queue line protocol is implemented by standalone Tcl
servers and retained by Omnivox for compatibility.

@item
Capability-negotiated Omnivox control@comma{} marker@comma{} logical-voice@comma{} and structured
presentation protocols carry bounded JSON records beside that legacy stream.
@end itemize

This chapter is primarily for speech-server and client developers.  The
detailed Omnivox contracts are owned by the Omnivox repository; this manual
summarizes their relationship to Emacsvox and then documents the legacy line
protocol.

For additional notes on how to log and view @acronym{TTS} server
commands when developing a speech server@comma{} see
the historical Emacspeak article at
@uref{http://emacspeak.blogspot.com/2015/04/howto-log-speech-server-output-to-aid.html}.

@section Omnivox Structured Protocol

At process startup the Emacsvox adapter requests capabilities and live engine
inventory.  Features are used only after negotiation; an older server
continues on the legacy path.  Current Emacsvox requires
@code{presentation_timeline_v3} before it submits an Aural Presentation as a
structured timeline.

A complete timeline contains its delivery policy@comma{} replacement domain@comma{}
generation@comma{} dispatch ID@comma{} speech spans@comma{} and positioned actions.  Small
timelines use one bounded frame; large timelines use a bounded multipart
envelope that is validated and admitted atomically.  Decodable rejected or
stale direct timelines receive terminal status so Emacs callbacks can retire
their dispatch records.

@code{ordered} and @code{urgent} timelines are admitted immediately.  Only
matching @code{replaceable} work uses the short server-side coalescing window.
Receipt of a newer valid replaceable generation immediately cancels queued@comma{}
synthesizing@comma{} and buffered work in the same replacement domain.  It does not
globally stop ordered@comma{} urgent@comma{} legacy@comma{} or differently keyed output.

Marker events distinguish dispatch admission@comma{} synthesis@comma{} playback start@comma{}
positioned markers@comma{} and terminal completion.  Playback markers track mixer
source consumption and may precede physical sound by the output device's
buffer latency; a protocol write or terminal callback is therefore not proof
of acoustic onset.

The canonical protocol documents are:

@itemize
@item
@uref{https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox/blob/main/CONTROL-PROTOCOL.md, Omnivox Control Protocol}

@item
@uref{https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox/blob/main/PRESENTATION-TIMELINE-PROTOCOL.md, Omnivox Presentation Timeline Protocol}

@item
@uref{https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox/blob/main/HELPER-PROTOCOL.md, Omnivox Helper Protocol}
@end itemize

@section Legacy Line Protocol

The @acronym{TTS} server reads commands from standard input.
Script @strong{speech-server} can be used to cause a @acronym{TTS}
server to communicate via a @acronym{TCP} socket. Speech server
commands are used by the client application to make specific requests
of the server; the server listens for these requests in a non-blocking
read-eval-print (REPL)  loop and executes requests as they become available. Requests can
be classified as follows:

@itemize
@item
Commands that send text to be spoken.

@item
Commands that set @strong{state} of the @acronym{TTS} server.
@end itemize

All commands are of the form

@example
commandWord @{arguments@}
@end example

The braces are optional if the command argument contains no white
space.  The speech server maintains a @strong{current state} that
determines various characteristics of spoken output such as speech
rate@comma{} punctuations mode etc. (see set of commands that manipulate
speech state for complete list).  The client application
@strong{queues} The text and non-speech audio output to be produced
before asking the server to @strong{dispatch} the set of queued
requests@comma{} i.e. start producing output.

Once the server has been asked to produce output@comma{} it removes items
from the front of the queue@comma{} sends the requisite commands to the
underlying @acronym{TTS} engine@comma{} and waits for the engine to
acknowledge that the request has been completely processed. This is a
non-blocking operation@comma{} i.e.@comma{} if the client application generates
additional requests@comma{} these are processed @strong{immediately}.

This design allows a legacy Emacsvox @acronym{TTS} server to be
@strong{highly} responsive; Client applications can queue large
amounts of text (typically queued a clause at a time to achieve the
best prosody)@comma{} ask the @acronym{TTS} server to start speaking@comma{} and
interrupt the spoken output at any time.

@subsection Testing A Direct Legacy Server

This protocol-level test is only for a retained direct Tcl-compatible server.
Run its launcher from the Emacsvox repository root@comma{} for example:

@example
./servers/espeak
@end example

Then enter:

@example
q @{This is a test.@}
d
s
@end example

The @code{q} command queues text@comma{} @code{d} dispatches it@comma{} and @code{s} stops speech.  Type
@kbd{C-d} to close the server.  A backend-specific README may provide a
stronger diagnostic and should take precedence over this generic protocol
test.

@subsection Commands That Queue Output.

This section documents commands that either produce spoken output@comma{} or
queue output to be produced on demand.  Commands that place the
request on the queue are clearly marked.

@example
version
@end example

Speaks the @strong{version} of the @acronym{TTS} engine. Produces
output immediately.

@example
tts_say text
@end example

Speaks the specified @strong{text} immediately. The text is not
pre-processed in any way@comma{} contrast this with the primary way of
speaking text which is to queue text before asking the server to
process the queue.

Note that this command needs to handle the special syntax for morpheme
boundaries @code{[*]}.  The @code{[*]} syntax is specific to the
Dectalk family of synthesizers; servers for other @acronym{TTS}
engines need to map this pattern to the engine-specific code for each
engine. As an example@comma{} see @code{servers/outloud} A morpheme boundary
results in synthesizing compound words such as @strong{left bracket}
with the right intonation; using a space would result in that phrase
being synthesized as two separate words.

@example
l c
@end example

Speak @strong{c} a single character@comma{} as a letter.  The character is
spoken immediately. This command uses the @acronym{TTS} engine's
capability to speak a single character with the ability to flush
speech @strong{immediately}.  Client applications wishing to produce
character-at-a-time output@comma{} e.g.@comma{} when providing character echo during
keyboard input should use this command.

@example
d
@end example

This command is used to @strong{dispatch} all queued requests.  It was
renamed to a single character command (like many of the commonly used
@acronym{TTS} server commands) to work more effectively over slow
(9600) dialup lines.  The effect of calling this command is for the
@acronym{TTS} server to start processing items that have been queued
via earlier requests.

@example
s
@end example

Stop speech @strong{immediately}.  Spoken output is interrupted@comma{} and
all pending requests are flushed from the queue.

@example
q text
@end example

Queues text to be spoken. No spoken output is produced until a
@strong{dispatch} request is received via execution of command
@strong{d}.

@example
c codes
@end example

Queues synthesis codes to be sent to the @acronym{TTS} engine.  Codes
are sent to the engine with no further transformation or processing.
The codes are inserted into the output queue and will be dispatched to
the @acronym{TTS} engine at the appropriate point in the output
stream.

@example
a filename
@end example

Cues the audio file identified by filename for playing@comma{}
an @code{ogg} file.

@example
p filename
@end example

@strong{dispatch} play the audio file identified by filename for
playing  @MDash{} @code{ogg} file.

@example
t freq length
@end example

Queues a tone to be played at the specified frequency and having the
specified length.  Frequency is specified in hertz and length is
specified in milliseconds.

@example
sh duration
@end example

Queues the specified duration of silence. Silence is specified in
milliseconds.

@subsection Commands That Set State

@example
tts_reset
@end example

@strong{Immediately} reset @acronym{TTS} engine to default settings.
Stops all speech and clears the queue.

@example
tts_set_punctuations mode
@end example

Queues setting @acronym{TTS} engine to the specified punctuation
mode. Typically@comma{} @acronym{TTS} servers provide at least three modes:

@itemize
@item
None: Do not speak punctuation characters.

@item
Some: Speak some punctuation characters. Used for English prose.

@item
All: Speak out @strong{all} punctuation characters; useful in
programming modes.
@end itemize

@example
tts_set_speech_rate rate
@end example

@strong{Immediately} change speech rate. The interpretation of this
value is typically engine specific.

@example
tts_set_character_scale factor
@end example

Queues changing the scale factor.  Scale factor applied to speech rate
when speaking individual characters.Thus@comma{} setting speech rate to 500
and character scale to 1.2 will cause command @strong{l} to use a
speech rate of @strong{500 * 1.2 = 600}.

@example
tts_split_caps flag
@end example

Queues changing of the state of @strong{split caps} processing.  Turn
this on to speak mixed-case (@acronym{AKA} Camel Case) identifiers.

@example
tts_sync_state punct splitcaps caps rate
@end example

@strong{Immediately} apply the passed settings.

This ensures @strong{atomicity} i.e.@comma{} all state settings in the TTS
engine happen at one shot. Note that failure to do this might result
in some utterances being spoken with a partially set state.

@itemize
@item
punct: see tts@math{_set}@math{_punctuations}

@item
splitcaps: see tts@math{_split}@math{_caps}

@item
caps: engine specific implementation of capital letter handling

@item
rate: see tts@math{_set}@math{_speech}@math{_rate}
@end itemize

@example
set_next_lang say_it
@end example

@strong{Immediately} switch to the next language on the server@comma{}
maintained internally on the TTS server. If say@math{_it} is non-nil say the
language change via the TTS@.

@example
set_previous_lang say_it
@end example

@strong{Immediately} switch to the previous language on the server@comma{}
maintained internally on the TTS server. If say@math{_it} is non-nil say the
language change via the TTS@.

@example
set_lang language:voice say_it
@end example

@strong{Immediately} switch to the requested language and/or voice@comma{}
separated by a colon. Examples

@itemize
@item
set@math{_lang} "en"

@item
set@math{_lang} "en:whisper"

@item
set@math{_lang} ":whisper"
@end itemize

If say@math{_it} is non-nil@comma{} speak via the TTS the language and voice
selected.

@example
set_preferred_lang alias lang
@end example

@strong{Immediately} set an alias in the TTS server mapping for
example "en" to "en@math{_GB}". This can later be used by set@math{_lang}.

